@startuml
' Figure - Use Case Diagram: what a Read-Only Viewer may do
!include _header.txt
left to right direction

actor "Read-Only Viewer" as RO

rectangle "Reading and monitoring" {
  usecase "Sign in"                  as A1
  usecase "Sign out"                 as A2
  usecase "Present second factor"    as A3
  usecase "Enrol second factor"      as A4
  usecase "Withdraw second factor"   as A5

  usecase "View own overview"        as R1
  usecase "List permitted cases"     as R2
  usecase "View a permitted case"    as R3
  usecase "View an evidence item"    as R4
  usecase "View custody log"         as R5
  usecase "View item audit trail"    as R6
  usecase "Run formula checks"       as R7
  usecase "Download custody report"  as R8

  usecase "View risk ranking"        as R9
  usecase "View item risk report"    as R10
  usecase "Read risk as data"        as R11
  usecase "View anomaly report"      as R12
  usecase "Ask the assistant"        as R13
  usecase "Open the assistant"       as R14
  usecase "Clear own conversation"   as R15
}

RO --> A1

A3 .> A1 : <<extend>>
RO --> A2
RO --> A3
RO --> A4
RO --> A5
RO --> R1
RO --> R2
RO --> R3
RO --> R4
RO --> R5
RO --> R6
RO --> R7
RO --> R8
RO --> R9
RO --> R10
RO --> R11
RO --> R12
RO --> R13
RO --> R14
RO --> R15

note right of R3
  Twenty use cases in total, and every one of
  them leaves the data unchanged. Each of R2
  to R8 is additionally confined to the cases
  this account is a member of.
end note

note right of R9
  These five are reachable and linked in the
  sidebar. They aggregate over the SAME
  permitted cases and write nothing.
end note

note bottom of A3
  Presenting a second factor EXTENDS signing in:
  it happens only when the account has one enrolled.
  Signing in is NOT <<include>>d by the other use
  cases - authentication is a precondition of the
  subject, not a step repeated inside each one.
end note

note bottom of A1
  The Read-Only Viewer is the BASE actor. Every
  other role specialises it and therefore inherits
  all twenty of these use cases, which is why they
  are drawn once here rather than repeated.
end note
@enduml
